+### [Manifold Dual Contouring - Cell Clustering](3677-manifold-dual-contouring-cell-clustering.md)
+Manifold Dual Contouring - Cell Clustering is the topological consolidation phase of the Manifold Dual Contouring algorithm, responsible for merging vertices from child octree nodes into a single manifold-compliant vertex per contour component within a parent node. It differs from standard dual contouring by enforcing manifold constraints through explicit vertex collapsing and edge processing, ensuring the resulting mesh is a valid 2-manifold without self-intersections or non-manifold edges. It builds on [Manifold Dual Contouring](2138-manifold-dual-contouring.md) by handling the adaptive octree structure's internal consistency.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-* The clustering phase dominates the computation time [S1, unofficial].
-* The extra computation time for enforcing the manifold criterion is negligible compared to QEF operations [S1, unofficial].
-* The method runs approximately an order of magnitude faster than Attali's method on iso-surfaces of similar complexity at the same grid resolution [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-* Hardware: 3 GHz Pentium computer with 2 GB RAM [S1, unofficial].
-* Model 1 (476,184 polygons): Clustering took 5.58 sec without manifold criterion and 5.76 sec with it [S1, unofficial]. Polygon generation took 1.12 sec, yielding 78 simplified polygons [S1, unofficial].
-* Model 2 (878,368 polygons): Clustering took 10.89 sec without manifold criterion and 10.99 sec with it [S1, unofficial]. Polygon generation took 2.01 sec, yielding 30,002 simplified polygons [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-* **Strengths:** Guarantees manifold surfaces preserving original topology; allows independent simplification of multiple contour components within a cell; no restriction on contour intersections per edge; efficient off-line polygon generation for varying viewer distances [S1, unofficial].
-* **Limitations:** May produce intersecting polygons despite being topologically manifold; requires Hermite data [S1, unofficial].
-
-**When to Use:**
-* Adaptive generation of iso-surfaces and real-time navigation of complex volumes, where QEF error thresholds are set higher in octree cells further from the viewer's location to produce more detailed geometry near the viewer and coarser polygons at distances [S1, unofficial].
